328:/// Verify that an already-built absolute path stays inside
329:/// `canonical_module_root` after symlink resolution. Used by call
330:/// sites that received their path from `safe_join` upstream and
331:/// want the F2 containment check without redoing the wire-path
332:/// validation. Same semantics as `contained_join` but takes the
333:/// already-resolved `target` directly.
334:///
335:/// af-4: a `..` component the walk has to pop — one beyond the deepest
336:/// existing ancestor — is refused. `canonicalize` cannot resolve it (its
337:/// prefix does not exist), the walk would drop it and judge the ancestor
338:/// alone, and at write time the OS would honour it: a target
339:/// `root/nonexistent/../../outside` passed as `root` and wrote above it.
340:/// A `..` inside the existing prefix is resolved by `canonicalize`
341:/// against the filesystem exactly as the OS will resolve it, and stays
342:/// accepted — a destination root typed as `../dst` reaches here on every
343:/// file.
344:///
345:/// A relative `target` is taken against the current directory, as the OS
346:/// will take it. Before af-4 a relative destination root never reached
347:/// this check at all: `canonical_dest_root` failed on it, the sink kept
348:/// no canonical root, and containment was silently skipped; now that the
349:/// root is captured, the target has to resolve the same way.
